WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.235
Lesson 3.4.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.350
Borrowing strategies and looping.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.133
By the end, you'll use borrowing deliberately,

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:09.502
and know exactly when looping adds real

00:00:09.502 --> 00:00:11.575
return, and when it just adds risk.

00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:14.165
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.165 --> 00:00:17.770
Borrowing for liquidity, without ever selling what you hold.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:22.315
Looping, mechanically, and the leverage formula it actually produces.

00:00:22.315 --> 00:00:25.344
Correlated loops versus volatile ones, and why that

00:00:25.344 --> 00:00:28.965
distinction matters more than the leverage number itself.

00:00:28.965 --> 00:00:32.304
And a full worked example, with real numbers, showing

00:00:32.304 --> 00:00:35.025
exactly when a loop's return gets wiped out.

00:00:36.325 --> 00:00:37.810
Liquidity without selling.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.736
Here's the actual use case borrowing solves,

00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:44.545
worth naming plainly before looping at all.

00:00:44.545 --> 00:00:47.300
You hold an asset you want to keep, long-term,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.590
but you need cash now, for something else entirely.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.320
Instead of selling, and losing your position, and potentially

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.111
triggering a taxable event, you borrow stablecoins against it,

00:00:58.111 --> 00:01:01.600
keep the asset locked as collateral, and repay later.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.574
Module twelve point three turns this exact

00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:07.410
habit into a real, written credit policy.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.104
Put a real example side by side with

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:15.415
the alternative, to make “liquidity without selling” concrete.

00:01:15.415 --> 00:01:18.441
Sell ten thousand dollars of an asset for cash,

00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:22.729
and you've lost that entire position, permanently, plus potentially

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:25.425
triggered a taxable event on any gain.

00:01:25.425 --> 00:01:28.386
Borrow five thousand dollars against that same ten

00:01:28.386 --> 00:01:31.288
thousand instead, and you keep the full position,

00:01:31.288 --> 00:01:33.785
still growing if the asset does, while the

00:01:33.785 --> 00:01:35.410
cash need is met either way.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:37.800
Quick check.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.567
What's the core benefit of borrowing stablecoins

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.825
against an asset, instead of selling it?

00:01:47.245 --> 00:01:50.096
The answer: you get liquidity now, without losing

00:01:50.096 --> 00:01:53.004
your position in the asset, and without triggering

00:01:53.004 --> 00:01:55.285
a sale you didn't actually want to make.

00:01:56.585 --> 00:01:57.255
Looping.

00:01:58.555 --> 00:02:01.287
Here's exactly what a loop does, mechanically,

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.040
one full cycle at a time.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.365
Deposit an asset as collateral.

00:02:05.365 --> 00:02:07.737
Borrow against it, up to some chosen

00:02:07.737 --> 00:02:10.045
L.T.V. Use what you borrowed to buy

00:02:10.045 --> 00:02:11.820
more of the same asset.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.607
And re-deposit that, as more collateral, starting the exact

00:02:15.607 --> 00:02:18.385
same cycle again, one loop deeper each time.

00:02:19.685 --> 00:02:22.132
Here's the formula that falls directly out

00:02:22.132 --> 00:02:24.466
of repeating that cycle, worth seeing in

00:02:24.466 --> 00:02:26.530
full before the worked example.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.422
After n loops at L.T.V. L, your leverage equals

00:02:30.422 --> 00:02:33.260
one minus L to the power of n plus

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.155
one, all divided by one minus L.

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:38.991
More loops push you closer and closer to

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:42.034
a ceiling; at L.T.V. zero point seven five,

00:02:42.034 --> 00:02:45.354
that ceiling is exactly four times leverage, no

00:02:45.354 --> 00:02:48.190
matter how many additional loops you run.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.726
Plot leverage against the number of loops, at

00:02:52.726 --> 00:02:55.540
an LTV of 0.7 specifically, and you can

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.006
watch that ceiling actually being approached.

00:02:59.006 --> 00:03:01.375
1 loop: 1.7x.

00:03:01.375 --> 00:03:06.560
3 loops: 2.53x, exactly this lesson's worked example.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.185
5 loops: 2.94x.

00:03:09.185 --> 00:03:12.736
And 10 loops: 3.27x, closing in on,

00:03:12.736 --> 00:03:17.075
but never quite reaching, the 3.33x ceiling.

00:03:18.376 --> 00:03:19.466
Quick check.

00:03:19.466 --> 00:03:23.716
What's the maximum possible leverage from looping at an

00:03:23.716 --> 00:03:26.751
LTV of 0.75, however many loops you run?

00:03:31.171 --> 00:03:34.741
The answer: four times, exactly one divided by one

00:03:34.741 --> 00:03:38.451
minus zero point seven five, the ceiling the formula

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.811
approaches as loops increase, but never exceeds.

00:03:43.111 --> 00:03:45.159
Correlated vs volatile loops.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:49.325
Here's the actual distinction that decides how

00:03:49.325 --> 00:03:51.824
dangerous any given loop really is, more

00:03:51.824 --> 00:03:54.624
than the leverage number alone ever does.

00:03:54.624 --> 00:03:57.852
A correlated loop, an L.S.T. against its own

00:03:57.852 --> 00:04:02.085
underlying asset, or one stablecoin against another, moves

00:04:02.085 --> 00:04:05.254
both sides together, almost in lockstep.

00:04:05.254 --> 00:04:09.060
A volatile-against-stable loop instead has collateral and debt

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.382
that can move in completely opposite directions, which

00:04:12.382 --> 00:04:15.644
is exactly what actually threatens your health factor.

00:04:16.944 --> 00:04:19.817
Worth naming concrete examples of each, so

00:04:19.817 --> 00:04:22.924
“correlated” isn't just an abstract label.

00:04:22.924 --> 00:04:27.558
A liquid-staking token looped against its own underlying, an

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:31.813
L.S.T. of E.T.H. against E.T.H. itself, is a correlated

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:36.519
loop; both sides track the same underlying price, closely.

00:04:36.519 --> 00:04:39.765
E.T.H. looped against U.S.D.C. debt is a

00:04:39.765 --> 00:04:44.674
volatile-against-stable loop; E.T.H.'s price can move sharply

00:04:44.674 --> 00:04:48.079
while the U.S.D.C. debt stays exactly flat.

00:04:49.379 --> 00:04:52.930
Here's exactly why that correlation matters so directly

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:56.464
to your safety, mechanically, not just intuitively.

00:04:56.464 --> 00:04:58.871
Health factor depends on your collateral's

00:04:58.871 --> 00:05:00.739
value relative to your debt.

00:05:00.739 --> 00:05:03.009
If both move together, in the same

00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:05.864
direction, by roughly the same amount, that

00:05:05.864 --> 00:05:09.018
ratio barely changes, even at high leverage.

00:05:09.018 --> 00:05:12.328
If they diverge instead, the exact same leverage

00:05:12.328 --> 00:05:16.178
multiple becomes dramatically more dangerous, far faster.

00:05:17.477 --> 00:05:18.482
Worked example.

00:05:19.782 --> 00:05:22.185
Here's the one relationship this entire

00:05:22.185 --> 00:05:24.347
lesson comes down to, visually.

00:05:24.347 --> 00:05:27.013
A leveraged loop's return depends entirely on

00:05:27.013 --> 00:05:29.737
the spread between what your collateral earns,

00:05:29.737 --> 00:05:32.007
and what your borrowed debt costs.

00:05:32.007 --> 00:05:34.007
Widen that spread, and leverage

00:05:34.007 --> 00:05:36.432
amplifies a genuinely good return.

00:05:36.432 --> 00:05:39.261
Narrow it, or let it flip negative, and the

00:05:39.261 --> 00:05:42.476
exact same leverage amplifies a loss just as fast.

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:47.261
Here's the setup this entire worked example runs on.

00:05:47.261 --> 00:05:50.519
LTV 0.7, 3 loops, 3.5%

00:05:50.519 --> 00:05:55.776
collateral yield, 2.5% borrow rate.

00:05:55.776 --> 00:05:59.054
Run through this program's own calculator,

00:05:59.054 --> 00:06:01.493
and leverage comes out to 2.53x.

00:06:02.793 --> 00:06:05.168
Now the net A.P.Y. on equity, step

00:06:05.168 --> 00:06:07.950
by step, the formula that actually tells

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.828
you whether this loop was worth doing.

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:14.027
Collateral yield, times leverage: three point five

00:06:14.027 --> 00:06:16.536
percent, times two point five three, is

00:06:16.536 --> 00:06:18.718
eight point eight six percent.

00:06:18.718 --> 00:06:21.528
Borrow rate, times leverage minus one: two

00:06:21.528 --> 00:06:24.208
point five percent, times one point five

00:06:24.208 --> 00:06:27.253
three, is three point eight three percent.

00:06:27.253 --> 00:06:30.796
Subtract the second from the first, and net A.P.Y.

00:06:30.796 --> 00:06:33.783
on equity is five point zero three percent.

00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:38.163
Now find the exact point where that positive return

00:06:38.163 --> 00:06:41.898
actually disappears, since rates don't stay fixed forever.

00:06:41.898 --> 00:06:44.397
At a two point five percent borrow

00:06:44.397 --> 00:06:46.895
rate, net A.P.Y. on equity is five

00:06:46.895 --> 00:06:50.408
point zero three percent, genuinely positive.

00:06:50.408 --> 00:06:53.333
Raise the borrow rate to five point seven eight

00:06:53.333 --> 00:06:56.806
percent, with nothing else changed, and that same loop's

00:06:56.806 --> 00:06:59.183
net return on equity hits exactly zero.

00:07:00.483 --> 00:07:03.560
And here's exactly why that break-even number should

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.172
worry you, not just interest you, given what

00:07:06.172 --> 00:07:08.678
Lesson three point one already showed.

00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:11.784
Borrow rates on a nearly-full pool can jump from

00:07:11.784 --> 00:07:15.713
around four percent to thirty-four percent, within hours, not

00:07:15.713 --> 00:07:20.133
months, exactly the kinked-curve mechanism from that same lesson.

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:23.084
A loop that looks comfortably profitable today can

00:07:23.084 --> 00:07:26.209
be underwater by tomorrow morning, with nothing about

00:07:26.209 --> 00:07:28.523
your own position having changed at all.

00:07:29.823 --> 00:07:30.913
Quick check.

00:07:30.913 --> 00:07:33.987
What single number actually decides whether a loop is

00:07:33.987 --> 00:07:36.948
worth doing, more than the leverage multiple itself?

00:07:41.368 --> 00:07:43.109
The answer: the spread between

00:07:43.109 --> 00:07:45.263
collateral yield and borrow rate.

00:07:45.263 --> 00:07:48.290
A wide, stable spread makes a loop worthwhile;

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:51.123
a narrow or shrinking one erases the return.

00:07:52.423 --> 00:07:55.586
Here's how to actually monitor an open loop, ongoing,

00:07:55.586 --> 00:07:58.053
not just at the moment you set it up.

00:07:58.053 --> 00:08:00.979
Check the current spread, collateral yield minus

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:03.367
borrow rate, on a regular schedule, not

00:08:03.367 --> 00:08:05.578
only when something feels wrong.

00:08:05.578 --> 00:08:09.437
Watch for utilisation climbing on the borrow side specifically,

00:08:09.437 --> 00:08:12.693
since that's the exact signal from Lesson three point

00:08:12.693 --> 00:08:14.983
one that rates are about to move.

00:08:14.983 --> 00:08:18.908
And unwind, deliberately, the moment the spread turns negative,

00:08:18.908 --> 00:08:21.483
rather than waiting to see if it recovers.

00:08:22.783 --> 00:08:24.243
Here's your checklist.

00:08:24.243 --> 00:08:26.498
Do it now, before opening any loop.

00:08:26.498 --> 00:08:29.165
Name your actual repayment source, before you

00:08:29.165 --> 00:08:31.553
ever borrow, not as an afterthought.

00:08:31.553 --> 00:08:35.507
Only loop correlated pairs, and stay below the mathematical

00:08:35.507 --> 00:08:39.196
maximum leverage for your L.T.V. And know your specific

00:08:39.196 --> 00:08:42.755
break-even borrow rate, with an alert actually set on

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:45.588
it, not just calculated once and forgotten.

00:08:46.888 --> 00:08:49.938
One last honest connection before the recap, tying

00:08:49.938 --> 00:08:53.108
looping directly back to Lesson three point two.

00:08:53.108 --> 00:08:56.615
Every loop still carries a health factor, exactly like

00:08:56.615 --> 00:09:00.378
any other borrow, and the exact same liquidation mechanics

00:09:00.378 --> 00:09:03.567
from Lesson three point three still apply, on the

00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:07.438
full, leveraged position, not just the original deposit.

00:09:07.438 --> 00:09:10.373
Looping doesn't create a new category of risk.

00:09:10.373 --> 00:09:12.198
It multiplies the familiar one.

00:09:13.498 --> 00:09:14.743
Let's recap.

00:09:14.743 --> 00:09:18.511
Borrowing against an asset gets you liquidity without selling

00:09:18.511 --> 00:09:21.668
it, a deliberate credit policy, not a reaction.

00:09:21.668 --> 00:09:27.149
Looping multiplies exposure through repeated deposit-borrow cycles, approaching

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:30.370
a leverage ceiling that never exceeds one over

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:34.001
one minus L.T.V. Correlated pairs keep health factor

00:09:34.001 --> 00:09:38.823
stable even at high leverage; volatile-against-stable pairs don't.

00:09:38.823 --> 00:09:41.763
And the spread between collateral yield and borrow

00:09:41.763 --> 00:09:44.817
rate, not the leverage number alone, decides whether

00:09:44.817 --> 00:09:47.123
any given loop was actually worth doing.

00:09:48.423 --> 00:09:51.947
Name your repayment source, loop only correlated pairs, and

00:09:51.947 --> 00:09:55.653
know your break-even borrow rate before you open anything.

00:09:55.653 --> 00:10:00.413
Next up, Lesson 3.5: Perpetual futures and margin on-chain.
